# forge-kit's allow-file for check-public-leaks.sh (issue #155).
#
# Four keys, and an unknown one refuses the whole run rather than being skipped: a silently
# ignored line in a security config is a guard reporting a coverage it does not have.
#
#   root ~/<root>   a home root this project's documents are allowed to show
#   prefix <path>   an absolute path under /home or /Users naming EXACTLY ONE segment, as in
#                   /home/<user>. Rule A matches one segment and nothing deeper, so a longer
#                   prefix could never match; it is refused rather than silently ignored.
#   email <addr>    an address that is deliberately published
#   skip <path>     a file excluded from the scan
#
# This file is scanned like any other, and it passes by construction: every entry allows the very
# string it is written with. Only the comments have to be written in the placeholder forms, which
# is why the line above says "~/<root>" rather than showing a name.
#
# This file is tracked and public on purpose. Everything in it is a thing the project has decided
# it may show, so publishing it discloses nothing. That is exactly why the IDENTITY half of the
# guard cannot work this way, and lives outside the repository instead.

# forge-kit documents itself as being cloned to a fixed location, so this is its canonical
# example root in the sense rule B asks for.
root ~/forge-kit

# An example of a path OUTSIDE the project, used by the overnight-guard fixtures to show what the
# hook refuses. It names no real directory.
root ~/data

# The author field in marketplace.json, which is published by design. Allowed as an address rather
# than by skipping the file, so the rest of marketplace.json stays scanned.
email a.gigante@gmail.com

# The scanner's contract test, which is the one place a real-shaped home path and a real-shaped
# address must appear as literals: the samples are what prove the patterns can fire. The scanner
# skips its own source without being asked; its test is skipped HERE, so the exemption is visible
# in config rather than hidden in the code.
skip scripts/test-check-public-leaks.sh

# check-private-leaks.sh's own contract test now carries an allow-file fixture (Task 3, forge-kit
# issue: a path allow-file for the private half) that includes a real-shaped root/prefix/email
# block, copied verbatim from the public suite's fixture above, to prove those keys are ignored
# rather than refused. Same reasoning, same exemption.
skip scripts/test-check-private-leaks.sh

# A real-shaped example user appears in this repository's plans when they quote rule A's boundary
# shapes (docs/plans/what-seventeen-repositories-found.md quotes four of them). The scanner's own
# comments use placeholder shapes since #223, so this entry rests on the plan file alone.
prefix /home/alice

# Historical doc roots (public --history): the project's own former name, and a <name> placeholder.
root ai-projectforge
root name>
# The history purge replaces a private root with [redacted]; rule B must not read that as a new root.
root [redacted]
# (the scanner strips trailing "]" from a match before comparing, so the stripped form is what matches)
root [redacted
